To count distinct subsequences, I’ll use dynamic programming. The key insight is that each character in S can either be included or not, leading to two possibilities for each position. I’ll maintain a 2D array dp where dp[i][j] represents the number of distinct subsequences ending with the jth character of S up to the ith position. The base case is dp[0][j] = 1 for all j, since the empty string has one subsequence. For each position i, I’ll update dp[i][j] by adding the number of subsequences ending with the jth character up to the (i-1)th position, which is dp[i-1][j]. If S[i] is equal to S[j], I’ll also add the number of subsequences ending with the jth character up to the (i-1)th position, which is dp[i-1][j]. This is because S[i] can be included in any subsequence ending with the jth character up to the (i-1)th position. Finally, I’ll return the sum of all dp[n-1][j] for all j, where n is the length of S, modulo 10^9 + 7.
